<h2 id="about">What this tool does</h2>
<div class="t3d-textbox"><p>Forms in Pakistan, the UAE and most visa portals ask for a photograph under a stated size — 20 KB for a CNIC upload, 50 KB for a passport application, 100 KB for a job portal, 200 KB for a university admission system. Guessing a quality setting to land inside that limit is tedious, so this tool searches for you: it re-encodes your picture repeatedly, adjusting JPEG quality and, if needed, the pixel dimensions, until the output actually fits the target you picked.</p><p>The search is a binary one, not a random one. Between quality 0.05 and 0.98 the browser's canvas encoder is asked for a middle value, the resulting blob is measured, and the range is narrowed — usually four or five attempts, all inside your device, all finished in under a second for a normal phone photo. You see each attempt tick past in the status line, then get the smallest file that is still no bigger than your target.</p><p>Prefer manual control? Switch the target to 'Quality slider only' and you get a conventional compressor with a live KB read-out, useful when a form has no size rule but you still want a light file for WhatsApp or email. WebP output is available too, which typically lands 25–35% smaller than JPEG at the same visual quality on modern browsers.</p></div>
<h2 id="how-to-use">How to use the Compress Image <span style="color:var(--cy)">| Step by Step Guide</span></h2>
<div class="howto"><ol><li><span>Drop the photo you need to shrink (JPG, PNG, WebP, BMP or HEIC; several at once is fine).</span></li><li><span>Choose a target: 20 KB, 50 KB, 100 KB, 200 KB, 500 KB — or 'No target' to use only the quality slider.</span></li><li><span>Leave 'Allow downscale' ticked for tight targets like 20 KB; untick it to keep every original pixel.</span></li><li><span>Press Compress and watch the status line as quality is searched automatically.</span></li><li><span>Save the result, compare the before/after sizes shown, or grab the whole batch as a ZIP.</span></li></ol></div>
<div class="t3d-textbox ru" data-ru lang="ros"><p>Photo dalain, target chunein — 20KB, 50KB, 100KB, 200KB ya 500KB. Tool khud quality kam zyada karke bilkul target ke barabar file bana deta hai, aur zaroorat parne par size bhi chhota karta hai. Compress dabakar download kar lein. Sab kuch aapke device me hota hai, upload koi nahi.</p></div>
<h2 id="features">Features</h2>
<div class="t3d-textbox"><ul class="feat-list"><li>Six real size targets plus a manual quality-slider mode</li><li>Binary quality search — finds the largest quality that still fits your limit</li><li>Optional controlled downscale when quality alone cannot reach the target</li><li>Before/after sizes and percentage saved shown for every file</li><li>Batch processing with one ZIP for portal uploads with several attachments</li><li>Transparent PNGs flattened on white so nothing turns black</li></ul></div>
<h2 id="tips">Tips</h2>
<div class="t3d-textbox"><ul><li>For a 20 KB CNIC photograph, allow downscaling to around 600 px — forms usually also demand small dimensions anyway.</li><li>Text-heavy screenshots compress poorly as JPEG; try PNG with 'No target' or crop out the background first.</li><li>If the form says 'maximum 100 KB' but rejects exactly 102,400 bytes, aim for the next target down for headroom.</li><li>Photos of documents get noisy: a slight crop of the dark desk area cuts size dramatically before you even compress.</li><li>Keep the original — re-saving repeatedly at lower quality accumulates artefacts that never come back.</li></ul></div>
<h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<details><summary>Why did it change my image dimensions?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>Only when quality alone could not reach the target and 'Allow downscale' was on. A 20 KB limit on a 12-megapixel photo is impossible without fewer pixels; the tool says so in the result note.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Is the compress image free to use?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>Yes — Compress Image has no usage cap, no sign-up and no watermark, and it is not a trial: the page is a static file on https://wafasmart.tech with no billing backend behind it. </p></div></details>
<details><summary>Does anything get uploaded when I use compress image?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>No. Compress Image reads your photo straight into page memory, works on it locally and hands the result back as a download — there is no upload request to intercept, store or leak, and no log of what you processed.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Can it make a PNG smaller without converting to JPG?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>PNG is lossless, so the honest options are fewer pixels or a palette reduction. This tool converts to JPG or WebP for real savings; use the Convert PNG to JPG page if you want that explicitly.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Which target do common portals need?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>Typical asks: NADRA/CNIC supporting documents 20–100 KB, passport photographs 50–200 KB, job-portal headshots 100 KB, university applications 200 KB. When unsure, pick the next smaller target — a rejected upload costs more time than a slightly softer photo.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Can I use it on my phone?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>Yes. Compress Image is mobile-first: one column, big tap targets, and the system keyboard opens with sensible number/text input. On iPhone, Files and the share sheet feed the picker the same way Downloads does on Android. </p></div></details>
